WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.240
JEN PERSON: Welcome to Getting
Started with Artifact Registry.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.160
In this video series,
I'm going to walk you

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.840
through setting
up some common use

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.150
cases for Artifact Registry.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.310
But before I get into
how to get started,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.880
it'd probably make sense to tell
you what Artifact Registry is.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.940
Artifact Registry manages your
container images and language

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.330
packages while integrating with
Cloud's Build, Test, and Deploy

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.450
suite and third
party CI/CD systems.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.790
If you're familiar with
Container Registry,

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.460
you're thinking Artifact
Registry sure sounds similar,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.660
and you're right.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.060
Artifact Registry is
the next generation

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.220
of Container Registry.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.620
Container Registry has
provided a single place

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.050
for your team to
manage docker images,

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.810
perform vulnerability
analysis, and decide

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.310
who can access what with
fine-grained access control.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.540
Existing CI/CD
integrations let you

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.620
set up fully automated Docker
pipelines to get fast feedback.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.770
In an effort to build upon
this powerful foundation,

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.740
Google Cloud created
Artifact Registry.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.130
Artifact registry offers even
more fine-grained permissions,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.459
allowing you to control access
at the project or registry

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:06.610
level.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.790
You can create multiple
regional repositories

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.640
within a single
Google Cloud project

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.220
you can create multiple
repos under a project

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.080
with independent
registry-specific permissions.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.860
So you have control over
where artifacts are stored

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.960
and who can access them.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.880
After hearing about
Artifact Registry,

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.275
I bet you can't
wait to get started.

00:01:26.275 --> 00:01:28.650
Let's walk through the steps
using a hypothetical company

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.050
I've created, Drayne.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.710
Drayne is autonomous,
artificially intelligent,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.450
voice-enabled plumbing
for the modern home.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.900
I'll make Drayne part
of your pipeline using

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.920
Artifact Registry in
the software pipeline.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:43.420
Now.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.420
If you don't think that's
a little bit of money,

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.640
this is going to be a
long video series for you.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.320
You can create a new repository,
list existing repositories,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.500
and configure access controls
through the GFP console

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.080
or through the gcloud
command-line tool.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.150
Today, I'm using gcloud.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.780
to find out about setting up the
G Cloud CLI check out the guide

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:04.930
link below.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.360
All right, let's get started.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.479
First, you'll need a
Google Cloud project

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:10.770
with billing enabled.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:14.510
You can use an existing
project or create a new one.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.240
Next, enable the Artifact
Registry API in the console,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.750
then open a new terminal window.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.810
To create a new repository,
type your gcloud command

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.440
in this format where
repository is the name

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.570
of your new repository.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.180
Format is the repository
format, which is either

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.730
Docker, Maven, or NPM.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.460
Location is the regional
or multi-regional location

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.720
for the repository.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.080
And you can leave aside if
you set a default location.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.490
And description is an optional
description of the repository.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.650
Including the async
flag causes the command

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.230
to return immediately without
waiting for the operation

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.860
in progress to complete.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.510
So for example, here's a
command from my Drayne app.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.905
My repository is called Drayne
Pipe, it's a Docker container,

00:03:01.905 --> 00:03:04.680
the Location I've
chosen is US Central-1,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.450
And I've included
this description

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.960
so I can easily track the
contents of my repositories.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.570
And here, is the results.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.520
To view a list of
repositories, you'll

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.930
want to run this command, where
project is the Project ID.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.510
If you don't include this flag,
then the current or default

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.100
project is used.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.620
Use the Location flag
to view repositories

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.730
from a specific location.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.750
You can also use Location
All to list repositories

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.730
across all locations.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.260
If you leave out
the location flag,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.330
the command uses
the default location

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.200
if a default is configured.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.350
And if there isn't
a default location,

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.330
leaving out this flag,
lists repositories

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.230
across all locations.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.520
So when I run the command
without a project or location,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.430
I'm shown my current
project here.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.250
As you can see, creating and
viewing your repositories

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.640
is super simple.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.880
But before you dive headlong
into Artifact Registry,

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.470
you have one very important
consideration, security.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.810
If you currently use
any GCP products,

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.850
you're probably at
least somewhat familiar

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.710
with the roles associated
with access controls.

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:12.620
The most basic rules are
owner, editor, and viewer.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.820
And although you can use these
primitive project-wide owner,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.110
editor, and viewer roles to
grant access to repositories,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.390
using specific
Artifact Registry roles

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.030
enables you to apply the
security principle of least

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.820
privilege so that users
and service accounts only

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.250
have access to the
permissions that are required.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.600
For example, the
artifactregistry.reader role

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.210
allows a user to view
and get artifacts

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.850
just from a specific
project's repositories.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.910
Whereas the roles/reader
role allows the user

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:45.570
to view and get any existing
resources and data from all

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.900
Cloud components of the project.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.710
To grant a permission
for a user,

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.830
naturally you grant a role
that includes that permission.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.740
Where appropriate you should
use the Artifact Registry

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.560
roles to control access to
your repositories, rather

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.250
than those project-wide roles.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:05.240
You can grant these Artifact
Registry roles, Reader, Writer,

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.200
Repository Administrator,
and Administrator.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.950
Let's grants some roles
for the repository

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.430
we just made for my completely
fake company, Drayne.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.840
Again, I'm using the gcloud
CLI because it rocks.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.380
To add roles via the CLI,
need to create a reference

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.440
policy file named policy.yaml.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.470
In that file, I start
with the bindings key,

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.250
then I name a role,
and list members.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:34.340
Here is a more complete
reference policy example.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:38.030
Notice I have an owner for the
whole project and then Artifact

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.280
Registry specific roles.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.720
For reader, you can
see there is a service

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.070
account with read privileges
and a specific user with read

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:47.680
privileges.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.430
You can find a complete
reference template

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.130
in the documentation,
which contains more example

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.410
user and service account names.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.240
To add a team
member to a project

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.700
and grant them an
Artifact Registry role,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.280
I add them to the policy file.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.970
Then, from the command
line, I run this command,

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:10.490
passing the name of the project
and the path to my .yaml file.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.770
To get the currently
configured policy,

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:16.400
I run gcloud project's
get-iam-policy, passing

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.360
the name of the project.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.450
And there you have it,
you're ready to create

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.870
a new repository, list
existing repositories,

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.080
and configure access controls
for Artifact Registry.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.870
But there's so much more to
know about Artifact Registry.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.300
Stay tuned for future
episodes of Getting

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.040
Started with Artifact Registry.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:37.490
And to see a guide on these
tasks and so much more,

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.082
check out the documentation.

00:06:39.082 --> 00:06:40.040
I'll see you next time.

